\section{Levels}{

A cell at LOD \code{r} summarises the leaves whose centres it contains, as
given by \code{\link[a5R:a5_cell_to_supercell]{a5R::a5_cell_to_supercell()}}, so each level partitions the
leaves, and \code{ncell} counts the leaves in each cell. Supercells do not
chain: a leaf's supercell two resolutions up is often not the supercell
of its supercell one resolution up. Each level is therefore grouped from
the leaves directly, never from the level below, and matches
\code{a5px::a5_aggregate()} of the leaves to that resolution.
}

\section{Reducers}{

Each value column is reduced over the leaves under each coarser cell by
its reducer, \code{aggregate} unless \code{reducers} names another:
\itemize{
\item \code{"mean"}: the mean, weighted by \code{weights} when given. For a column of
codes with a codec (see "Codecs"), the code of the mean of the decoded
values.
\item \code{"sum"}, \code{"min"}, \code{"max"}: the sum, minimum or maximum.
\item \code{"mode"}: the most frequent value, by total weight when \code{weights} is
given, ties going to the smallest value. For category codes.
\item \code{"rep_child"}: the value of the representative leaf, the leaf closest
to the cell's mean over all \code{"rep_child"} columns, each divided by its
standard deviation so that units do not matter. Every \code{"rep_child"}
column takes its value from the same leaf, so a coarse cell keeps a
combination of values that occurs in the data, such as an RGB colour,
rather than a blend. The standard deviations are computed over the
leaves unless \code{rep_sd} fixes them, and are recorded in the index
(\code{\link[=a5_pyramid_info]{a5_pyramid_info()}}).
}

Missing values are skipped, and a cell whose leaves are all missing is
\code{NA}. The \code{weights} column, when selected, is summed unless \code{reducers}
says otherwise. Results do not depend on the number of threads.
}

\section{Vector columns}{

A \emph{vector column} holds a fixed number of 8-bit values per cell, such as
an embedding: an integer matrix column of a data frame (one row per
cell), or a fixed-size list of \code{int8} or \code{uint8} (or fixed-size binary,
